// t-seq-03: plain seq's trigger sits under the machine's catch, so an
// error the trigger raises aborts the run in progress.
//
// desugar_plain (graphix-types/src/expr/seq.rs:313-318) pushes the
// machine's catch before `let go = filter(<trigger>, |x| x ~ idle)`, so a
// `?` in the trigger (or in a function the trigger calls) raises to the
// machine's handler: every running step's guard fails and the abort
// action writes pc idle. seqq binds its trigger in the prelude outside the
// machine (desugar_queued, seq.rs:920-925), and seq's own abort(..) event
// is also bound before the catch (seq.rs:310-312).
//
// Here go fires at step 1 (run 1 starts, c = 1, waits for step > 11) and
// at step 5, where e is an error. The step-5 trigger arrives while run 1
// is busy, so it should be dropped (design/seq_blocks.md section 5,
// book/src/core/seq.md "seq and seqq": the forms differ only in drop vs
// queue) and its error should reach the enclosing handler.
//
// command: graphix --no-cache design/review-2026-10-05/repro/t-seq-03.gx
//          (same with --no-fusion; graphix-fuzz check: AGREE)
//
// expected: "run 1 started", then 1 (and the trigger's error reported).
//   `seqq let` in place of `seq let` prints exactly that; so does this
//   program with e never an error (`99 => error(`T)`).
// observed: "run 1 started", the unhandled-error report, and no value:
//   run 1 was killed at step 5.
//
// Same-cycle variant: with `go = select step { 5 => step, _ => never() }`
// and the trigger `({ e?; go })`, the trigger fires and raises at step 5;
// the start write (pc <- go ~ S0) and the abort action's pc <- Idle queue
// one after the other, so the run prints "run 5 started" and is killed a
// cycle later; seqq prints "run 5 started" then 5.
let step = 0;
step <- select step { n if n < 40 => n + 1, _ => never() };
let e: [i64, Error<`T>] = select step { 5 => error(`T), n => n };
let go = select step { 1 | 5 => step, _ => never() };
let r = seq let c = (go ~ e)? {
    println("run [c] started");
    until step > c + 10;
    c
};
sys::exit(sys::time::after_idle(duration:300.ms, 0));
r
